Most common and impactful AKS issue :

🔍 Issue: Pods Stuck in Pending State

✅ Symptom

When you run kubectl get pods, you see something like:

NAME                       READY   STATUS    RESTARTS   AGE
my-app-746fbc4476-mc2ph   0/1     Pending   0          3m



🎯 Root Causes

1. No available nodes with enough resources (CPU/Memory) to schedule the pod.
2. Node selectors or taints are preventing pods from being scheduled.
3. Insufficient node pool capacity or misconfigured autoscaler.
4. Incorrect storage class or PVC pending for volume attachments.



🧪 Step-by-Step Debugging

1. Check the pod description

kubectl describe pod <pod-name>

👉 Look under the Events: section for clues. You might see something like:

Warning  FailedScheduling  default-scheduler  0/3 nodes are available: 3 Insufficient cpu.

2. Check node availability and resource usage

kubectl get nodes
kubectl describe node <node-name>
kubectl top nodes

👉 If nodes are running low on CPU/memory, your pod cannot be scheduled.

3. Check pod resource requests and limits

resources:
  requests:
    memory: "512Mi"
    cpu: "1"

👉 If a pod requests more than any node can provide, it stays pending.

4. Check taints and tolerations

kubectl describe node <node-name> | grep Taint

👉 Taints like key=value:NoSchedule block pods unless they tolerate them.

5. Check if PVC is pending

kubectl get pvc
kubectl describe pvc <pvc-name>

👉 PVCs stuck in Pending mean the volume isn’t being provisioned.



💡 Solution

Scenario A: Insufficient Resources

Option 1: Manually scale up node pool

az aks nodepool scale \
  --resource-group <rg> \
  --cluster-name <cluster> \
  --name <nodepool-name> \
  --node-count 5

Option 2: Reduce pod resource requests

resources:
  requests:
    cpu: "0.5"
    memory: "256Mi"


Scenario B: Taints / Node Selectors

Add a toleration to your pod spec:

tolerations:
- key: "example"
  operator: "Equal"
  value: "true"
  effect: "NoSchedule"

Or remove unnecessary taints from node:

kubectl taint nodes <node-name> example=true:NoSchedule-


Scenario C: PVC Not Bound

Ensure correct StorageClass is used:

storageClassName: managed-premium

Validate the StorageClass exists:

kubectl get storageclass

Use static provisioning or fix volume access modes if needed.



✅ Final Validation

Once fixed:

kubectl get pods

You should see:

my-app-746fbc4476-mc2ph   1/1     Running   0          10s
